# 001300-001399/20250914T134839Z_1311285336.wiki
zΔ⚡b|=5bc6|+%3D Extensions %3D%3D%3D%0AThere are some modifications and extensions to the diff formats that are used and understood by certain programs and in certain contexts. For example, some [[revision control]] systems—such as [[Subversion (software)%7CSubversion]]—specify a version number, "working copy", or any other comment instead of or in addition to a timestamp in the diff's header section.%0A%0ASome tools allow diffs for several different files to be merged into one, using a header for each modified file that may look something like this:%0A%0A Index: path/to/file.cpp%0A%0AThe special case of files that do not end in a newline is not handled. Neither {{code%7Cunidiff}} nor the POSIX {{code%7Cdiff}} standard define a way to handle this type of files. (Indeed, such files are not "text" files by strict POSIX definitions.<ref>http://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799/basedefs/V1_chap03.html#tag_03_403 {{Webarchive%7Curl%3Dhttps://web.archive.org/web/20130429195728/http://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799/basedefs/V1_chap03.html#tag_03_403 %7Cdate%3D2013%2D04%2D29 }} Section 3.206</ref>) GNU diff and git produce "\ No newline at end of file" (or a translated version) as a diagnostic, but this behavior is not portable.<ref>{{cite web %7Ctitle%3DIncomplete Lines (Comparing and Merging Files) %7Curl%3Dhttps://www.gnu.org/software/diffutils/manual/html_node/Incomplete%2DLines.html %7Cwebsite%3Dwww.gnu.org}}</ref> GNU patch does not seem to handle this case, while git%2Dapply does.<ref>{{cite web %7Ctitle%3Dgit: apply.c %7Curl%3Dhttps://github.com/git/git/blob/69c786637d7a7fe3b2b8f7d989af095f5f49c3a8/apply.c#LL2901C35%2DL2901C39 %7Cpublisher%3DGit %7Cdate%3D8 May 2023}}</ref>%0A%0AThe [[Patch (Unix)%7Cpatch]] program does not necessarily recognize implementation%2Dspecific diff output. GNU patch is, however, known to recognize git patches and act a little differently.<ref>{{cite web %7Ctitle%3Dpatch.c\src %2D patch.git %2D GNU patch %7Curl%3Dhttps://git.savannah.gnu.org/cgit/patch.git/tree/src/patch.c?id%3Dc835ecc67b7e37c0d0b7dd7e032209fdaa285808#n1919 %7Cwebsite%3Dgit.savannah.gnu.org %7Cquote%3DIn git%2Dstyle diffs, the "before" state of each patch refers to the initial state before modifying any files,..}}</ref>%0A%0A%3D%3D|=3b07|
# 001300-001399/20251012T215644Z_1316506009.wiki
zΔ⚡b|=4a4f|-1173|=437a|
# 001300-001399/20251012T215825Z_1316506247.wiki
zΔ⚡b|=4a4f|+ is often used as input to the [[patch (Unix)%7Cpatch]] program. Many projects specifically request that "diffs" be submitted in the unified format, making unified diff format the most common format for exchange between software developers.%0A%0AUnified context diffs were originally developed by Wayne Davison in August 1990 (in '''unidiff''' which appeared in Volume 14 of comp.sources.misc). [[Richard Stallman]] added unified diff support to the [[GNU%7CGNU Project]]'s diff one month later, and the feature debuted in '''GNU diff''' 1.15, released in January 1991. GNU diff has since generalized the context format to allow arbitrary formatting of diffs.%0A%0AThe format starts with the same two%2Dline [[Header (computing)%7Cheader]] as the context format, except that the original file is preceded by "<samp>%2D%2D%2D</samp>" and the new file is preceded by "<samp>%2B%2B%2B</samp>". Following this are one or more '''change hunks''' that contain the line differences in the file. The unchanged, contextual lines are preceded by a space character, addition lines are preceded by a [[plus sign]], and deletion lines are preceded by a [[minus sign]].%0A%0AA hunk begins with '''range information''' and is immediately followed with the line additions, line deletions, and any number of the contextual lines. The range information is surrounded by double [[at sign]]s, and combines onto a single line what appears on two lines in the context format ([[#Context format%7Cabove]]). The format of the range information line is as follows:%0A%0A @@ %2Dl,s %2Bl,s @@ ''optional section heading''<!%2D%2D <syntaxhighlight lang%3D"text"> ruins the italics %2D%2D>%0A%0AThe hunk range information contains two hunk ranges. The range for the hunk of the original file is preceded by a minus symbol, and the range for the new file is preceded by a plus symbol. Each hunk range is of the format ''l,s'' where ''l'' is the starting line number and ''s'' is the number of lines the change hunk applies to for each respective file. In many versions of GNU diff, each range can omit the comma and trailing value ''s'', in which case ''s'' defaults to 1. Note that the only really interesting value is the ''l'' line number of the first range; all the other values can be computed from the diff.%0A%0AThe hunk range for the original should be the sum of all contextual and deletion (including changed) hunk lines. The hunk range for the new file should be a sum of all contextual and addition (including changed) hunk lines. If hunk size information does not correspond with the number of lines in the hunk, then the diff could be considered invalid and be rejected.%0A%0AOptionally, the hunk range can be followed by the heading of the section or function that the hunk is part of. This is mainly useful to make the diff easier to read. When creating a diff with GNU diff, the heading is identified by [[regular expression]] matching.<ref>[https://www.gnu.org/software/diffutils/manual/html_node/Sections.html 2.2.3 Showing Which Sections Differences Are in], GNU diffutils manual</ref>%0A%0AIf a line is modified, it is represented as a deletion and addition. Since the hunks of the original and new file appear in the same hunk, such changes would appear adjacent to one another.<ref>[http://www.artima.com/weblogs/viewpost.jsp?thread%3D164293 Unified Diff Format] by [[Guido van Rossum]], June 14, 2006</ref> An occurrence of this in the example below is:%0A%0A<pre>%0A%2Dcheck this dokument. On%0A%2Bcheck this document. On%0A</pre>%0A%0AThe command <code>diff %2Du original new</code> produces the following output:%0A<syntaxhighlight lang%3D"diff">%0A%2D%2D%2D /path/to/original%09timestamp%0A%2B%2B%2B /path/to/new%09timestamp%0A@@ %2D1,3 %2B1,9 @@%0A%2BThis is an important%0A%2Bnotice! It should%0A%2Btherefore be located at%0A%2Bthe beginning of this%0A%2Bdocument!%0A%2B%0A This part of the%0A document has stayed the%0A same from version to%0A@@ %2D8,13 %2B14,8 @@%0A compress the size of the%0A changes.%0A%0A%2DThis paragraph contains%0A%2Dtext that is outdated.%0A%2DIt will be deleted in the%0A%2Dnear future.%0A%2D%0A It is important to spell%0A%2Dcheck this dokument. On%0A%2Bcheck this document. On%0A the other hand, a%0A misspelled word isn't%0A the end of the world.%0A@@ %2D22,3 %2B23,7 @@%0A this paragraph needs to%0A be changed. Things can%0A be added after it.%0A%2B%0A%2BThis paragraph contains%0A%2Bimportant new additions%0A%2Bto this document.%0A</syntaxhighlight>%0A%0ATo successfully separate the file names from the timestamps, the delimiter between them is a tab character. This is invisible on screen and can be lost when diffs are copy/pasted from console/terminal screens.|=437a|
